Choosing between Redux, Context API, and TanStack Query (React Query) depends on your application's needs. Here's a breakdown of when to use each:

1. Redux (State Management)
When to Use:

Global state that changes frequently (e.g., user auth, shopping cart, complex UI state)

Large-scale apps where state needs to be predictable and debuggable

Middleware needs (e.g., Redux Thunk for async logic, Redux Saga for side effects)

Time-travel debugging (Redux DevTools)

Shared state across many components (avoiding prop drilling)

Example Apps:

E-commerce platforms (cart, user session, product filters)

Dashboards with real-time updates

Complex forms with multi-step validation

Apps requiring undo/redo functionality

2. Context API (React Built-in)
When to Use:

Simple global state (e.g., theme, language preference)

Avoiding prop drilling in medium-sized apps

Static or rarely updated state (performance is better than Redux for this case)

Small to medium apps where Redux is overkill

Limitations:

Not optimized for high-frequency updates (re-renders all consumers)

No built-in middleware or devtools

Can become messy if overused for complex state

Example Apps:

Theme toggling (light/dark mode)

User authentication (simple cases)

Localization (language switching)

Small apps with minimal shared state

3. TanStack Query (React Query)
When to Use:

Server-state management (data fetching, caching, background updates)

Auto-revalidation (stale-while-revalidate pattern)

Optimistic updates (UI updates before server confirmation)

Pagination, infinite scroll, and dependent queries

Reducing Redux boilerplate (if state is mostly server data)

Example Apps:

Social media feeds (auto-refreshing posts)

Dashboards with real-time data (stocks, analytics)

Apps with heavy API interactions

Apps needing offline-first capabilities (with caching)

Comparison Table
Tool	              Best For	                                   Not Ideal For
Redux	          Complex client-side state	                   Simple apps / server state
Context API	  Simple global state, theming	                  High-frequency updates
TanStack Query	  Server state, API caching	                Local-only state

Possible Combinations
Redux + TanStack Query

Use Redux for UI state (e.g., modals, forms).

Use TanStack Query for API data (avoid storing fetched data in Redux).

Example: E-commerce app (Redux for cart, React Query for products).

Context API + TanStack Query

Use Context for theme/auth.

Use React Query for data fetching.

Example: Blog site (Context for dark mode, React Query for posts).

Redux Alone

Only if you need middleware (thunks/sagas) and complex state.

Example: A real-time collaborative editor.

TanStack Query Alone

If most state is server data (no need for Redux).

Example: A news aggregator with infinite scroll.

Final Recommendation
Use Context API for simple, static global state (theme, auth).

Use TanStack Query for server data (API calls, caching).

Use Redux only for complex client-side state (forms, real-time UI).

For most modern apps, TanStack Query + Context API covers 90% of cases, while Redux is needed only for very complex state logic.


